scribes unbounded growth of a sequence index?","options":["$n\\to\\infty$","$n = \\infty$","$\\infty \\lt n$","$n\\in\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"1dd8edbb31ffc97b1fb1f072"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which notation indicates positive infinity as a direction?","options":["$x = +\\infty$","$+\\infty\\in\\mathbb{R}$","$1 \\lt +\\infty$","$x\\to+\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"26ab71f2d4af567abb41f3c1"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why should one not treat $\\infty$ as an ordinary real number?","options":["A symbol for unboundedness, not a real.","It is equal to every real number.","It is the smallest real number","It is a hidden variable"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"04dae1ee04ab94e943253af2"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"When must the direction of infinity be specified explicitly?","options":["Whenever a natural number is used.","Only when a function is constant","When a variable may diverge to $\\pm\\infty$.","Only for finite intervals"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"b7b766482cbe84f1cc1afec8"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression correctly uses $+\\infty$ as a limiting behaviour?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = 0$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = +\\infty$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = 1$","$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = -\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"91a667b78832a6d9e2f5d1a4"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_008","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which notation best describes a function value decreasing without bound?","options":["$f(x) = -\\infty$ as a real value","$-\\infty \\lt f(x)$ as an ordinary real comparison","$f(x)\\to-\\infty$","$f(x)\\in-\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"e3c73dcc3a0d9a404fb97a6a"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_009","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which statement avoids the misconception warned about?","options":["$1<+\\infty$ is not a real inequality.","$+\\infty$ is a real number larger than $1$.","$\\infty$ is the maximum of $\\mathbb{R}$","$-\\infty$ is a negative real number"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c4831d8fa360e3a3c3b75465"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_010","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What distinguishes $n\\to\\infty$ from $x\\to+\\infty$?","options":["$n\\to\\infty$ has implicit direction; $x\\to+\\infty$ states it.","$n\\to\\infty$ means $n$ equals a real number","$x\\to+\\infty$ means $x$ is fixed","There is no role for direction in either notation."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f84b1298b74f8d33bb7eb113"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_011","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"A student writes $x = +\\infty$ to describe unbounded increase. Which replacement best matches the context?","options":["$x\\in+\\infty$","$x\\to+\\infty$","$+\\infty\\to x$","$x \\lt +\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d0b4fbcc5520fb129a5962d5"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_012","difficulty":"challenge","question_type":"application","question":"Which statement is the safest interpretation of $\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to 0^+}\\frac{1}{x} = +\\infty$?","options":["The function equals the real number $+\\infty$ at $x = 0$.","The function is bounded above near $0$","The function grows without bound as $x\\to0^+$.","The function becomes a finite constant near $0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f644397b366240e5c998f36c"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_013","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Is $\\infty$ a real number in this context?","options":["Yes, it is the largest real number","Yes, it is a natural number","Only when it appears in a limit","No, it is a symbol or idea for unboundedness"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"fc7ff7e4335a0047b8533735"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_014","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In $(a,+\\infty)$, why is $+\\infty$ not an element of the interval?","options":["It is included because the parenthesis is open.","It marks an unbounded direction, not a real endpoint.","It equals the largest real number.","It is included only when $a=0$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"06b2c98398bca277bc3d54d2"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"In $(-\\infty,b)$, what does $-\\infty$ indicate?","options":["The interval contains a smallest real number called $-\\infty$.","The interval is unbounded to the left.","The interval is empty.","Only negative integers are allowed."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"361f67d1e8691d245b805043"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which notation describes $x$ increasing without bound?","options":["$x=+\\infty$","$x\\to-\\infty$","$x\\to+\\infty$","$x \\lt +\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"a241c46be6abda7574a4c984"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which notation describes $x$ decreasing without bound?","options":["$x\\to-\\infty$","$x=0$","$x\\to+\\infty$","$x \\lt -\\infty$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ffcdfb3568812b16057b9064"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_018","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"Which statement correctly describes $n\\to\\infty$?","options":["The index becomes the largest real number","The index is fixed at $0$","The sequence index grows without bound","The index decreases without bound"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"ed63d40f5c9e1dec001963c0"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_019","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Why is writing $1 \\lt +\\infty$ discouraged here?","options":["It uses the wrong inequality symbol for real numbers","It means $1$ is negative","It treats $+\\infty$ as if it were a real number","It makes $+\\infty$ finite"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"56c0204ec42da14779aa5ffc"},{"id":"year_1_1a_math_i_calculus_infinity_b9201b03_020","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Which expression correctly uses infinity to describe unbounded limit behaviour?","options":["$\\displaystyle\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\frac{1}{x}=+\\infty$","$\\displaystyle\\frac{1}{0}=+\\infty$ as a real-number equality","$1 \\lt +\\infty$","$x=\\infty$ for some real $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"036bae78bf7ce78c483f14c1"}]}}